title "Dijkstra shortest paths"
layout layered dir=down
page a3
font 8pt..36pt wrap=17em

style input color=#1565C0
style step color=#2E7D32
style result color=#6A1B9A

node graph "Weighted graph" .input
| directed or undirected graph G = (V, E)
| every edge has weight w(u,v) >= 0
| negative edges require another algorithm
node source "Choose source" .input
| initialize d[source] = 0
| initialize every other distance to infinity
node invariant "Greedy invariant" .step
| settled d[u] equals the true shortest distance
| only when all edge weights are non-negative
node queue "Priority queue" .step
| smallest tentative distance first
| keyed by tentative distance d[v]
node relax "Relax edges" .step
| d[v] = min(d[v], d[u] + w(u,v))
| update predecessor when the bound improves
node settle "Settle vertex" .step
| remove the minimum; its distance is final
| each vertex is settled at most once
node repeat "Repeat" .step
| inspect every outgoing edge of the settled vertex
| stop when the queue is empty or target is settled
node paths "Shortest paths" .result
| predecessor links reconstruct each route
| unreachable vertices retain distance infinity
node correctness "Correctness proof" .result
| induction on the order vertices are settled
| a shorter route would cross the frontier
node complexity "Complexity" .result
| binary heap: O((|V| + |E|) log |V|)
| adjacency lists make sparse graphs efficient
node failure "Failure modes" .result
| negative edge: greedy choice may be wrong
| overflow: infinity plus a weight must be guarded
node compare "Related algorithms" .result
| BFS is Dijkstra with unit edge weights
| Bellman-Ford handles negative edges

graph -> source
source -> invariant
invariant -> queue
queue -> relax
relax -> settle
settle -> repeat
repeat -> queue
repeat -> paths
settle -> correctness
queue -> complexity
relax -> failure
failure -> compare

require paths at bottom
prefer repeat at right weight=2
prefer correctness near invariant within=18em weight=3
prefer compare near failure within=18em weight=2
